<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Gerard of Cremona</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 1114–<a href="/years/1187">1187</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/12th-century">12th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Gerard of Cremona was an Italian scholar who worked in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/toledo">Toledo</a> and translated scores of Arabic scientific and philosophical works into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a>. His translations included Ptolemy's <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/almagest">Almagest</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/avicenna">Avicenna</a>'s <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/the-canon-of-medicine">Canon of Medicine</a>, works of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/euclid">Euclid</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aristotle">Aristotle</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/al-khwarizmi">al-Khwarizmi</a>, and the surgery of al-Zahrawi. They made him the most productive of the twelfth-century translators who transmitted Greek and Arabic learning to Latin <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/gerard-of-cremona.jpg" alt="Gerard of Cremona" width="320" height="364" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Gerard of Cremona · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Al-RaziInGerardusCremonensis1250.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Gerard was born in Cremona in northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a>. Dissatisfied with the philosophies of his Italian teachers, he went to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/toledo">Toledo</a>, and no detailed information survives on when, though it was no later than 1144. He went to learn Arabic so that he could read Ptolemy's Almagest, which had a high reputation among scholars but was not yet known in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> translation in the West. Toledo had been a provincial capital in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caliphate">Caliphate</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cordoba">Cordoba</a> and remained a seat of learning. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alfonso-vi-of-castile">Alfonso VI of Castile</a> conquered it from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moors">Moors</a> in 1085, which made it safely available to a Catholic.</p><p>Its rulers protected the large Jewish and Muslim quarters, and the city stayed an important centre of Arab and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hebrew">Hebrew</a> culture, with Mozarabic culture common in the area. It was full of libraries and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manuscript">manuscripts</a>, and was one of the few places in medieval <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a> where a Christian could be exposed to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arabic-language">Arabic language</a> and culture. One of the great scholars associated with it was Rabbi <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abraham">Abraham</a> ibn <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ezra">Ezra</a>, Gerard's contemporary.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>In Toledo Gerard devoted the rest of his life to making <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> translations from Arabic scientific literature. He is regarded as the most important translator of the Toledo School of Translators, which transmitted Arab and ancient Greek knowledge in astronomy, medicine and other sciences to Western medieval <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a> in the 12th century. Some of the books he obtained in the Toledo libraries had been written in Greek and were well known in Byzantine Constantinople but unavailable in Greek or Latin in the West.</p><p>In all he translated 87 books from Arabic. The historian Edward Grant ranks him above every other western translator and says that his translations alone would have drastically altered the course of western science.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Works and ideas</h2><p>His <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> version of the Arabic Almagest, made about 1175, was the most widely known in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/western-europe">Western Europe</a> before the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/renaissance">Renaissance</a>, although an earlier translation from the Greek had been made in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sicily">Sicily</a> about 1160 under Henricus Aristippus. The Almagest formed the basis for Western astronomy until Copernicus. Gerard also edited the Tables of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/toledo">Toledo</a>, partly the work of al-Zarqali of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cordoba">Cordoba</a>, and rendered al-Farabi's book on the sciences as De scientiis.</p><p>His other translations include <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/euclids-elements">Euclid's Elements</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/archimedes">Archimedes</a>' On the Measurement of the Circle, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aristotle">Aristotle</a>'s On the Heavens, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/al-khwarizmi">al-Khwarizmi</a>'s On Algebra and Almucabala, Jabir ibn Aflah's Elementa astronomica and works of al-Razi. He also composed original treatises on algebra, arithmetic and astrology, and created anatomical terms. A second translator, Gerard de Sabloneta, worked in the 13th century and is probably responsible for some works credited to Gerard.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Gerard of Cremona” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Al-Zahrawi’s treatise on surgery was translated into Latin by <b>Gerard of Cremona</b> and used in European medicine for centuries.</blockquote><a href="/empire/caliphate-of-cordoba">Caliphate of Córdoba · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>…city of al-Andalus to fall, and in Toledo, where Archbishop Raymond (1125–1152) encouraged the work, scholars such as <b>Gerard of Cremona</b> and Dominicus Gundissalinus translated Arabic works of philosophy, astronomy and medicine into Latin, including Ptolemy's Almagest and works of Avicenna and al-Farabi, transmitting them to the schools…</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-castile">Kingdom of Castile · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>Scholars writing in Arabic, from Baghdad to Córdoba, translated, criticised and extended Greek science, and in the 12th century translators such as <b>Gerard of Cremona</b> at Toledo turned Arabic texts into Latin, among them the Almagest and many works of Arabic science.</blockquote><a href="/movement/scientific-revolution">Scientific Revolution · Background</a></li><li><blockquote>The Canon was translated from Arabic into Latin as Canon medicinae by a <b>Gerard of Cremona</b>; there appear to have been two men of that name, both translators of Arabic texts into Latin.</blockquote><a href="/work/the-canon-of-medicine">The Canon of Medicine · Survival &amp; Rediscovery</a></li><li><blockquote>In the twelfth century scholars in Spain and Sicily translated Arabic works into Latin; <b>Gerard of Cremona</b>, working in Toledo, alone rendered dozens of texts, among them Ptolemy's Almagest.</blockquote><a href="/essays/cross-cultural-exchange">Cross-Cultural Exchange</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Cremona, in what is now <a href="/countries/italy">Italy</a>. Died in Toledo, in what is now <a href="/countries/spain">Spain</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=45.13,10.03&amp;year=1161" aria-label="Cremona on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4788 570 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Gerard of Cremona"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5238 840h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5238 840h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=45.13,10.03&amp;year=1161">What was here in 1161? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
